tasks.brief:
A TV remote sends only the arrow keys, Enter (its OK button) and Back. Today the page can be used with a mouse or a finger only. Create ONE new file, public/tvnav.js, and load it from public/index.html. This task: arrow keys move a visible highlight (real keyboard focus) to the nearest thing in that direction, and Enter presses it. (The guide's own rules come in T02, Back and the player in T03: do not write them now.)

A. public/index.html - one change only. The file ends with `</script>\n</body>\n</html>`. Insert one line so it ends with:
</script>
<script src="/tvnav.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
Do not read the rest of the file (3,000 lines); you need nothing else from it except the ids named below.

B. public/tvnav.js - plain browser JavaScript (no modules, no libraries), everything inside one `(function(){ 'use strict'; ... })();`, two-space indents, `var` and `function`. The page's own script has already run when this file loads, so its globals exist (isTvBrowser, toggleOptions, CHANNELS ...). Write these parts:

1. State and helpers:
   var on = false;      // remote mode
   var lastApp = null;  // the last thing highlighted on the guide screen
   var SELECTOR = 'button, a[href], input, select, video, [tabindex], .fs-item, .prog-cell, .chip, .icon-btn, .avatar, .detail-chanlogo';
   function $(id){ return document.getElementById(id); }
   function isOpen(id){ var e = $(id); return !!e && e.classList.contains('open'); }
   function rect(el){ return el.getBoundingClientRect(); }

2. shown(el) -> can a person see and use it? false when: no el, !el.isConnected, el.disabled; its rect has width <= 0 or height <= 0; getComputedStyle(el) has visibility 'hidden' or pointerEvents 'none' (that is how the hidden Browse column is known); el.closest('[hidden]'); or it is inside #detail-drawer while that drawer does not have the class 'open'. Otherwise true.

3. scope() -> the layer the remote is working in, as a list of root elements; only things inside can be reached. First match wins:
   #gate without the class 'hidden' -> [gate];  #subs-modal open -> [it];  #playnow-modal open -> [it];
   #player-modal open -> [#social-panel, #player-modal] when #social-panel is open too, else [#player-modal];
   #options-dropdown open -> [it];  otherwise [#app].   ("open" = has the class 'open'.)
   inScope(el) -> true when some root contains el.   inGuide(el) -> !!el && !!el.closest && !!el.closest('#guide-rows').

4. candidates() -> every element matching SELECTOR inside the scope roots that is shown(), EXCEPT: anything inside .chan-fav-controls (favourite stars stay a mouse feature); an <a> with target "_blank" (a TV cannot open another window); and, inside the guide, anything that is not on screen. On screen in the guide: with sc = rect(#guide-scroll) and r = rect(el): r.bottom > sc.top + 30 && r.top < sc.bottom && r.right > sc.left && r.left < sc.right.

5. highlight(el) -> if !el return false. A div cannot take focus: if el has no tabindex attribute and its tagName is not BUTTON, A, INPUT or SELECT, set tabindex="-1". Then el.focus({ preventScroll: true }); el.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', inline: 'nearest' }); return true.

6. nearest(cur, dir, list) -> the best element of list in direction dir ('up','down','left','right') from cur, or null. Use exactly this (it was tuned against the real page):

  function nearest(cur, dir, list){
    var a = rect(cur), best = null, bestScore = Infinity;
    var acx = (a.left + a.right) / 2, acy = (a.top + a.bottom) / 2;
    list.forEach(function(el){
      if(el === cur) return;
      var b = rect(el), bcx = (b.left + b.right) / 2, bcy = (b.top + b.bottom) / 2, gap, off;
      if(dir === 'left' || dir === 'right'){
        if(dir === 'right' ? !(bcx > acx && b.right > a.right) : !(bcx < acx && b.left < a.left)) return;
        gap = Math.max(0, dir === 'right' ? b.left - a.right : a.left - b.right);
        off = (b.bottom > a.top && b.top < a.bottom) ? 0 : Math.abs(bcy - acy);
      } else {
        if(dir === 'down' ? !(bcy > acy && b.bottom > a.bottom) : !(bcy < acy && b.top < a.top)) return;
        gap = Math.max(0, dir === 'down' ? b.top - a.bottom : a.top - b.bottom);
        off = (b.right > a.left && b.left < a.right) ? 0 : Math.abs(bcx - acx);
      }
      var score = gap + off * 3 + (off ? 0 : Math.abs(dir === 'left' || dir === 'right' ? bcy - acy : bcx - acx) / 10);
      if(score < bestScore){ bestScore = score; best = el; }
    });
    return best;
  }

7. firstChoice() -> where the highlight starts when nothing is highlighted: if the scope is #app: lastApp when it is still connected and shown(); else the first .guide-row inside #guide-rows that does not have the class row-hidden -> its `.prog-cell.on-now`, or its `.logo-tile` when there is none. In every other case: candidates()[0] || null.

8. move(dir): cur = document.activeElement. If there is none, or it is document.body, or !inScope(cur), or !shown(cur): highlight(firstChoice()) and return. Otherwise next = nearest(cur, dir, candidates()); if next, highlight(next). (When there is nothing in that direction the highlight stays.)

9. turnOn(): once only: on = true; document.body.classList.add('tv-nav').

10. Keys - ONE listener: document.addEventListener('keydown', function(e){...}, true) (the `true` matters: it runs before the video's and the pickers' own key handling). Ignore the event when e.altKey, e.ctrlKey or e.metaKey. cur = document.activeElement.
   - typing(el): el is a TEXTAREA, or an INPUT whose type is not checkbox, radio, button or submit.
   - e.key ArrowUp/ArrowDown/ArrowLeft/ArrowRight -> dir. If typing(cur) and dir is left or right: return without doing anything (the caret moves inside the text box). Otherwise e.preventDefault(); turnOn(); move(dir).
   - e.key 'Enter' or ' ': if !on, or no cur, or cur is body, or typing(cur): return. If cur is a BUTTON, A, INPUT or SELECT, or has the class logo-tile: return (they answer Enter themselves). Otherwise e.preventDefault(); cur.click().

11. Follow the highlight: document.addEventListener('focusin', function(e){ var el = e.target; if(!$('app').contains(el) || scope()[0] !== $('app')) return; lastApp = el; });

12. The visible highlight: create a <style> element and append it to document.head with exactly:
   body.tv-nav :focus{outline:3px solid #4CE0E8 !important; outline-offset:2px;}
   body.tv-nav .prog-cell:focus, body.tv-nav .fs-item:focus, body.tv-nav #player-video:focus{outline-offset:-3px;}
   body.tv-nav .prog-cell:focus{background:#1E2C63 !important; color:#F4F5F9;}

13. Last lines inside the function: window.tvNav = { isOn: function(){ return on; }, turnOn: turnOn }; and: if (typeof isTvBrowser === 'function' && isTvBrowser()) turnOn();  (on a TV browser remote mode is on from the start; on a computer it turns on at the first arrow key, so nothing changes for mouse users).

Check the file with `node --check public/tvnav.js`. Run .venv\Scripts\python.exe tests\test_remote_basics.py (about a minute and a half: it walks the whole screen with the arrow keys in a headless browser). The two sign-in tests must still pass.

The guide is a grid: one row per channel (a logo, then its programmes along a time line that scrolls sideways). "Nearest thing" is wrong there: a remote must move programme by programme along a channel, and channel by channel up and down while staying at the same time of day. Only public/tvnav.js changes. Keep everything T01 made; add and change the following.

The guide's structure (already in the page): #guide-scroll is the scrolling box. Inside it, .guide-ruler-row is the time ruler (it sticks to the top). #guide-rows holds one .guide-row per channel (data-row = the channel id; class row-hidden when a filter hides it). In a row: .chan-cell (it sticks to the left edge) containing .logo-tile, and a .timeline-track containing the .prog-cell elements, absolutely placed by time.

1. New state next to `on`: var anchorX = null; (the screen x kept while moving up and down, so the column does not drift) and var lastSpot = null; (which guide cell to highlight again after the guide redraws: {chan, logo, x}).

2. Helpers:
   guideRows() -> the .guide-row elements of #guide-rows without the class row-hidden, as an array (use it in firstChoice too).
   rowLogo(row) -> row.querySelector('.logo-tile').
   rowCells(row) -> its .prog-cell elements as an array sorted by offsetLeft.
   stickyRight(row) -> the right edge of its .chan-cell: rect(row.querySelector('.chan-cell')).right.
   visibleX(cell) -> the middle of the part of the cell a person can see: left = max(rect(cell).left, stickyRight(row)); right = min(rect(cell).right, rect(#guide-scroll).right); return right > left ? (left + right) / 2 : rect(cell).left + 4.
   cellAt(row, x) -> the cell of that row whose rect contains x (left <= x <= right); when none contains it, the one whose nearer edge is closest to x; null when the row has no cells.

3. revealInGuide(el) -> scroll #guide-scroll so the highlight is on screen. Use exactly this:

  function revealInGuide(el){
    var sc = $('guide-scroll'), s = rect(sc), row = el.closest('.guide-row'), r = rect(row);
    var ruler = sc.querySelector('.guide-ruler-row'), top = ruler ? rect(ruler).bottom : s.top;
    if(r.top < top) sc.scrollTop -= (top - r.top);
    else if(r.bottom > s.bottom) sc.scrollTop += (r.bottom - s.bottom);
    if(el.classList.contains('prog-cell')){
      var c = rect(el), left = stickyRight(row);
      if(c.left > s.right - 120) sc.scrollLeft += c.left - left - 40;      // starts off the right edge: bring its start in
      else if(c.right < left + 40) sc.scrollLeft -= (left + 40 - c.right) + Math.min(c.width - 40, 300);
    }
    var v = rect(el);
    if(v.bottom > window.innerHeight || v.top < 0) el.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', inline: 'nearest' });
  }

4. remember(el) and spotElement():
  function remember(el){
    if(!inGuide(el)){ if($('app').contains(el)) lastSpot = null; return; }
    var row = el.closest('.guide-row');
    var isLogo = el.classList.contains('logo-tile');
    lastSpot = { chan: row.dataset.row, logo: isLogo, x: isLogo ? 0 : (anchorX != null ? anchorX : visibleX(el)) };
  }
  function spotElement(){
    if(!lastSpot) return null;
    var row = document.querySelector('#guide-rows .guide-row[data-row="' + lastSpot.chan + '"]');
    if(!row || row.classList.contains('row-hidden')) return null;
    return lastSpot.logo ? rowLogo(row) : (cellAt(row, lastSpot.x) || rowLogo(row));
  }

5. moveInGuide(cur, dir) -> true when the key was used up inside the guide, false when the highlight should leave the guide. Use exactly this:

  // -> true when the key was used up inside the guide
  function moveInGuide(cur, dir){
    var row = cur.closest('.guide-row'), isLogo = cur.classList.contains('logo-tile');
    if(dir === 'left' || dir === 'right'){
      anchorX = null;
      var items = [rowLogo(row)].concat(rowCells(row));
      var i = items.indexOf(cur) + (dir === 'right' ? 1 : -1);
      if(i < 0) return false;                         // left of the logo: out to the Browse column, if it is showing
      if(i < items.length) highlight(items[i]);
      return true;
    }
    var rows = guideRows(), j = rows.indexOf(row) + (dir === 'down' ? 1 : -1);
    if(j < 0) return false;                           // above the first channel: out to the buttons over the guide
    if(j >= rows.length) return true;
    // a logo moves to a logo; but a logo reached while walking a column (an empty channel) keeps the column
    if(isLogo && anchorX == null){ highlight(rowLogo(rows[j])); return true; }
    if(anchorX == null) anchorX = visibleX(cur);
    highlight(cellAt(rows[j], anchorX) || rowLogo(rows[j]));
    return true;
  }

6. Changes to T01's functions:
   - highlight(el): after the focus() call, replace the scrollIntoView line with: `if(inGuide(el)) revealInGuide(el); else if(el.scrollIntoView) el.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', inline: 'nearest' });` and then `remember(el);` (after the scrolling, so the place is remembered as the person sees it).
   - move(dir): after the "nothing highlighted" check add `if(inGuide(cur) && moveInGuide(cur, dir)) return;`. Then: `var list = candidates(); if(inGuide(cur)) list = list.filter(function(el){ return !inGuide(el); });` (leaving the guide must not land on another guide cell) and `var next = nearest(cur, dir, list); if(next){ anchorX = null; highlight(next); }`.
   - firstChoice(): in the #app case use `(lastApp && lastApp.isConnected && shown(lastApp)) ? lastApp : spotElement()` before falling back to the first row.
   - the focusin listener: after `lastApp = el;` add `remember(el);`.

7. The guide redraws itself every minute (renderGrid() empties #guide-rows and builds new elements), which destroys the highlighted cell. Put the highlight back:
   new MutationObserver(function(){
     if(!on || !lastSpot || scope()[0] !== $('app')) return;
     var cur = document.activeElement;
     if(cur && cur !== document.body && cur.isConnected) return;
     var el = spotElement();
     if(el){ var keep = anchorX; highlight(el); anchorX = keep; }
   }).observe($('guide-rows'), { childList: true });

Run .venv\Scripts\python.exe tests\test_remote_guide.py. tests\test_remote_basics.py and the sign-in tests must still pass.

Last part of the remote: Back, the player, and a full-screen picture on a TV. Only public/tvnav.js changes. Keep everything T01 and T02 made. The page's own functions used here already exist as globals: closeSubtitleSearch(), closePlayNow(), closeSocialPanel(), closePlayer(), toggleOptions(), closeDetail(), cancelSignIn(), playChannel(id, name), isTvBrowser(), and the variables CHANNELS (array of {id, name}) and currentChannelId.

1. isTvFirst() -> typeof isTvBrowser === 'function' && isTvBrowser().  Use it wherever T01 wrote that test.

2. The player (#player-modal, open = class 'open'; the picture is <video id="player-video">):
   - changeChannel(step): ids = guideRows().map(row -> row.dataset.row); if empty return. i = ids.indexOf(String(currentChannelId)); the new id is ids[((i < 0 ? 0 : i + step) + ids.length) % ids.length] (it wraps round at both ends); find that channel in CHANNELS (compare String(c.id)) and call playChannel(chan.id, chan.name).
   - playPause(): var v = $('player-video'); if (v.paused) { var p = v.play(); if (p && p.catch) p.catch(function(){}); } else { v.pause(); }
   - in move(dir), right after the "nothing highlighted" check: if cur.id === 'player-video': for up or down call changeChannel(dir === 'down' ? 1 : -1) and return; for left or right highlight document.querySelector('#player-modal button[onclick="closePlayer()"]') (the Close button) and return.
   - at the end of move(dir): when nearest() found nothing, dir is 'up' and #player-modal contains cur: highlight($('player-video')).  (Up from the row of buttons returns to the picture.)
   - firstChoice(): when scope()[0] is #player-modal or #social-panel return $('player-video').
   - in the keydown listener: in the Enter/space branch, before the "answers Enter itself" test: if cur.id === 'player-video' { e.preventDefault(); playPause(); return; }. New branch: e.key 'MediaPlayPause', 'MediaPlay' or 'MediaPause' -> if the player is open: e.preventDefault(); playPause().

3. Back. tvBack() closes the top-most open thing and returns true; it returns false when nothing was open (the TV app then leaves). Use exactly this:

  function tvBack(){
    var gate = $('gate');
    if(gate && !gate.classList.contains('hidden')){
      if(!$('link-panel').hidden && !isTvFirst()){ cancelSignIn(); return true; }
      return false;
    }
    if(isOpen('subs-modal')){ closeSubtitleSearch(); return back(); }
    if(isOpen('playnow-modal')){ closePlayNow(); return back(); }
    if(isOpen('player-modal')){
      if(isOpen('social-panel')){ closeSocialPanel(); highlight($('player-video')); return true; }
      closePlayer();
      return back();
    }
    if(isOpen('options-dropdown')){ toggleOptions(); return back(); }
    if(isOpen('detail-drawer')){
      var spot = spotElement();
      closeDetail();
      if(on) highlight(spot || firstChoice());
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
  function back(){
    if(on){ var cur = document.activeElement; if(!inScope(cur) || !shown(cur)) highlight(firstChoice()); }
    return true;
  }
   In the keydown listener add: e.key 'Escape', 'GoBack' or 'BrowserBack', or 'Backspace' while not typing(cur) -> if (tvBack()) e.preventDefault();
   And near the end: window.tvBack = tvBack;

4. Two more observers (next to the one for #guide-rows):
   - the player opening and closing:
     new MutationObserver(function(){
       if(!on) return;
       if(isOpen('player-modal')){ if(!$('player-modal').contains(document.activeElement)) highlight($('player-video')); }
       else if(!inScope(document.activeElement)){ highlight(firstChoice()); }
     }).observe($('player-modal'), { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['class'] });
   - the info drawer opening: the page puts the focus on Watch Live, which on a small screen scrolls the title out of sight. Observe #detail-drawer the same way (attributes, class): if (!on || !isOpen('detail-drawer')) return; then setTimeout(function(){ var v = document.querySelector('.view'); if (v && isOpen('detail-drawer')) v.scrollTop = 0; }, 400);

5. On a TV the picture fills the screen, and the channel line and the buttons lie over its bottom edge and fade away after five seconds without a key:
   var idleTimer = null;
   function wake(){ document.body.classList.remove('tv-idle'); clearTimeout(idleTimer); idleTimer = setTimeout(function(){ document.body.classList.add('tv-idle'); }, 5000); }
   document.addEventListener('keydown', wake, true);
   Add these rules to the <style> element's text, exactly:
   body.tv-device .player-modal{padding:0; gap:0;}
   body.tv-device .player-modal .player-stage{width:100vw; height:100vh; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center;}
   body.tv-device .player-modal video{width:100%; height:100%; max-width:none; max-height:none; border-radius:0; object-fit:contain;}
   body.tv-device .player-modal .player-info, body.tv-device .player-modal > div:last-child{position:absolute; bottom:12px; z-index:8; transition:opacity .4s ease;}
   body.tv-device .player-modal .player-info{left:14px; background:rgba(8,10,15,.75); border-radius:10px; padding:6px 10px;}
   body.tv-device .player-modal > div:last-child{right:14px;}
   body.tv-device.tv-idle .player-modal .player-info, body.tv-device.tv-idle .player-modal > div:last-child{opacity:0;}
   The last line of the file's function becomes: if(isTvFirst()){ document.body.classList.add('tv-device'); turnOn(); wake(); }

Run .venv\Scripts\python.exe tests\test_remote_player.py (about a minute). All the older tests must still pass.
